{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794546175455,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","part":{"id":"prt_1d3498ddf001JL9Dbh2DewCiQ6","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","messageID":"msg_1d34952d6001WWg3jQehSp3lgy","type":"step-start","snapshot":"506a235ea56935572b47ea58ee639fc1c17877e0"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546179031,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3499a630018zF6xLEVhokjj9","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","messageID":"msg_1d34952d6001WWg3jQehSp3lgy","type":"tool","id":"call_ae32ad74f0f349f0ab3635aa","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-offer.vitest.ts"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-offer.vitest.ts, lines 1-336\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Command offer\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: \n5: // The head is the only advisor text that names both the offered command and the\n6: // policy, so these cases pin each shape a live head takes, a command with a\n7: // policy, a command under an ambiguous head without one, and a head that names\n8: // no command at all. The replay case proves a checkout without the prompt hook\n9: // reports the miss, because a backfill off such a checkout has to stay readable\n10: // instead of throwing away the lines around it. The delivery cases pin the two\n11: // real answers a Claude transcript can give and the third answer a runtime with\n12: // no confirmed delivery point must give, so a later summary never reads a\n13: // missing record as a miss. The backfill cases pin that a line keeps its place\n14: // when its checkout is gone, that a refused row leaves no output behind, and\n15: // that the new log never replaces the one it reads.\n16: \n17: import { existsSync, mkdirSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, realpathSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n18: import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\n19: import { join } from 'node:path';\n20: \n21: import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\n22: \n23: import {\n24:   backfillRunLog,\n25:   briefDelivered,\n26:   codexHookApproval,\n27:   parseOffer,\n28:   replayOffer,\n29: } from '../scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs';\n30: \n31: const OFFERED_HEAD = 'Advisor: live; use sk-git → /doctor:git hooks (ask) 0.82/0.12 pass.';\n32: const AMBIGUOUS_HEAD = 'Advisor: live; ambiguous: /doctor:git hooks 0.82/0.12 vs sk-git 0.86/0.16 pass.';\n33: const NO_COMMAND_HEAD = 'Advisor: live; use command-speckit-plan 0.82/0.16 pass.';\n34: const CODEX_HOOKS = {\n35:   hooks: {\n36:     UserPromptSubmit: [\n37:       { hooks: [{ command: 'node x/hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js' }] },\n38:     ],\n39:   },\n40: };\n41: \n42: function writeTranscript(lines: unknown[]): { dir: string; transcriptPath: string } {\n43:   const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n44:   const transcriptPath = join(dir, 'session.jsonl');\n45:   writeFileSync(transcriptPath, lines.map((line) => JSON.stringify(line)).join('\\n'));\n46:   return { dir, transcriptPath };\n47: }\n48: \n49: function writeRunLog(dir: string, lines: unknown[]): string {\n50:   const inPath = join(dir, 'runs.jsonl');\n51:   writeFileSync(inPath, lines.map((line) => JSON.stringify(line)).join('\\n') + '\\n');\n52:   return inPath;\n53: }\n54: \n55: function writeCodexRoot(withHooks: boolean): { dir: string; hooksPath: string } {\n56:   const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-codex-'));\n57:   const hooksPath = join(dir, '.codex', 'hooks.json');\n58:   if (withHooks) {\n59:     mkdirSync(join(dir, '.codex'), { recursive: true });\n60:     writeFileSync(hooksPath, JSON.stringify(CODEX_HOOKS));\n61:   }\n62:   return { dir, hooksPath };\n63: }\n64: \n65: function writeCodexConfig(dir: string, lines: string[]): string {\n66:   const configPath = join(dir, 'config.toml');\n67:   writeFileSync(configPath, lines.map((line) => `${line}\\n`).join(''));\n68:   return configPath;\n69: }\n70: \n71: describe('parseOffer', () => {\n72:   it('reads the command and the policy a head offers', () => {\n73:     expect(parseOffer(OFFERED_HEAD)).toEqual({\n74:       offeredCommand: '/doctor:git hooks',\n75:       offeredPolicy: 'ask',\n76:     });\n77:   });\n78: \n79:   it('reads the command but no policy under an ambiguous head', () => {\n80:     expect(parseOffer(AMBIGUOUS_HEAD)).toEqual({\n81:       offeredCommand: '/doctor:git hooks',\n82:       offeredPolicy: null,\n83:     });\n84:   });\n85: \n86:   it('reads no command when the head names none', () => {\n87:     expect(parseOffer(NO_COMMAND_HEAD)).toEqual({\n88:       offeredCommand: null,\n89:       offeredPolicy: null,\n90:     });\n91:   });\n92: });\n93: \n94: describe('replayOffer', () => {\n95:   it('reports a checkout without the prompt hook instead of throwing', () => {\n96:     const checkoutDir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n97:     try {\n98:       expect(replayOffer({ checkoutDir, prompt: 'create a readme', sessionId: 'offer-test' })).toEqual({\n99:         offeredCommand: null,\n100:         offeredPolicy: null,\n101:         offerError: 'hook_missing',\n102:       });\n103:     } finally {\n104:       rmSync(checkoutDir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n105:     }\n106:   });\n107: });\n108: \n109: describe('codexHookApproval', () => {\n110:   it('reports an advisor hook that no config entry approves as unapproved', () => {\n111:     const { dir } = writeCodexRoot(true);\n112:     try {\n113:       const configPath = writeCodexConfig(dir, []);\n114:       expect(codexHookApproval({ projectDir: dir, configPath })).toBe('unapproved');\n115:     } finally {\n116:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n117:     }\n118:   });\n119: \n120:   it('reports an advisor hook approved by a trusted hash as approved', () => {\n121:     const { dir, hooksPath } = writeCodexRoot(true);\n122:     try {\n123:       const configPath = writeCodexConfig(dir, [\n124:         `[hooks.state.\"${realpathSync(hooksPath)}:user_prompt_submit:0:0\"]`,\n125:         'trusted_hash = \"x\"',\n126:       ]);\n127:       expect(codexHookApproval({ projectDir: dir, configPath })).toBe('approved');\n128:     } finally {\n129:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n130:     }\n131:   });\n132: \n133:   it('reports a root with no hooks file as no-hooks', () => {\n134:     const { dir } = writeCodexRoot(false);\n135:     try {\n136:       expect(codexHookApproval({ projectDir: dir })).toBe('no-hooks');\n137:     } finally {\n138:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n139:     }\n140:   });\n141: });\n142: \n143: describe('briefDelivered', () => {\n144:   it('finds the advisor context in a Claude transcript attachment', async () => {\n145:     const { dir, transcriptPath } = writeTranscript([\n146:       { type: 'user', message: { role: 'user', content: 'create a readme' } },\n147:       {\n148:         type: 'attachment',\n149:         attachment: {\n150:           type: 'hook_additional_context',\n151:           hookEvent: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n152:           content: [OFFERED_HEAD],\n153:         },\n154:       },\n155:     ]);\n156:     try {\n157:       await expect(briefDelivered({ runtime: 'claude', locator: { transcriptPath } })).resolves.toBe(true);\n158:     } finally {\n159:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n160:     }\n161:   });\n162: \n163:   it('returns false when the Claude transcript holds no advisor attachment', async () => {\n164:     const { dir, transcriptPath } = writeTranscript([\n165:       { type: 'user', message: { role: 'user', content: 'create a readme' } },\n166:       { type: 'attachment', attachment: { type: 'hook_additional_context', hookEvent: 'SessionStart', content: ['Session ready'] } },\n167:     ]);\n168:     try {\n169:       await expect(briefDelivered({ runtime: 'claude', locator: { transcriptPath } })).resolves.toBe(false);\n170:     } finally {\n171:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n172:     }\n173:   });\n174: \n175:   it('returns unknown for a runtime with no confirmed delivery point', async () => {\n176:     await expect(briefDelivered({\n177:       runtime: 'devin',\n178:       locator: { transcriptPath: join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-devin.jsonl') },\n179:     })).resolves.toBe('unknown');\n180:   });\n181: \n182:   it('returns false for a Codex hook the config never approved', async () => {\n183:     const { dir } = writeCodexRoot(true);\n184:     try {\n185:       const configPath = writeCodexConfig(dir, []);\n186:       await expect(briefDelivered({\n187:         runtime: 'codex',\n188:         locator: { projectDir: dir },\n189:         codexConfig: configPath,\n190:       })).resolves.toBe(false);\n191:     } finally {\n192:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n193:     }\n194:   });\n195: });\n196: \n197: describe('backfillRunLog', () => {\n198:   it('adds the offer and the brief to every line and warms each checkout once', async () => {\n199:     const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n200:     const outPath = join(dir, 'runs-out.jsonl');\n201:     const inPath = writeRunLog(dir, [\n202:       { runtime: 'claude', rowId: 'row-one', arm: 'pre', locator: { projectDir: '/checkout-one' }, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n203:       { runtime: 'claude', rowId: 'row-two', arm: 'pre', locator: { projectDir: '/checkout-one' }, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n204:     ]);\n205:     const before = readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8');\n206:     const warmed: string[] = [];\n207:     const replayed: Array<{ checkoutDir: string; prompt: string; sessionId: string }> = [];\n208:     const seen: Array<{ runtime: string; locator: unknown }> = [];\n209:     try {\n210:       const result = await backfillRunLog({\n211:         inPath,\n212:         outPath,\n213:         promptFor: (rowId: string) => `prompt for ${rowId}`,\n214:         warm: async (checkoutDir: string) => {\n215:           warmed.push(checkoutDir);\n216:         },\n217:         replay: async ({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId }: { checkoutDir: string; prompt: string; sessionId: string }) => {\n218:           replayed.push({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId });\n219:           return { offeredCommand: '/doctor:git hooks', offeredPolicy: 'ask', offerError: null };\n220:         },\n221:         brief: async ({ runtime, locator }: { runtime: string; locator: unknown }) => {\n222:           seen.push({ runtime, locator });\n223:           return true;\n224:         },\n225:       });\n226: \n227:       expect(result).toEqual({ ok: true, lines: 2, outPath });\n228:       const written = readFileSync(outPath, 'utf8').trim().split('\\n').map((line) => JSON.parse(line));\n229:       expect(written).toHaveLength(2);\n230:       expect(written[0]).toMatchObject({\n231:         rowId: 'row-one',\n232:         offeredCommand: '/doctor:git hooks',\n233:         offeredPolicy: 'ask',\n234:         offerError: null,\n235:         briefDelivered: true,\n236:         offerBackfilled: true,\n237:       });\n238:       expect(written[1].rowId).toBe('row-two');\n239:       expect(warmed).toEqual(['/checkout-one']);\n240:       expect(replayed.map((call) => call.sessionId)).toEqual(['backfill-row-one', 'backfill-row-two']);\n241:       expect(replayed[0]).toMatchObject({ checkoutDir: '/checkout-one', prompt: 'prompt for row-one' });\n242:       expect(seen).toHaveLength(2);\n243:       expect(readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8')).toBe(before);\n244:     } finally {\n245:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n246:     }\n247:   });\n248: \n249:   it('marks a line whose locator names no checkout instead of replaying it', async () => {\n250:     const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n251:     const outPath = join(dir, 'runs-out.jsonl');\n252:     const inPath = writeRunLog(dir, [\n253:       { runtime: 'codex', rowId: 'row-no-checkout', arm: 'pre', locator: null, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n254:     ]);\n255:     let warmed = 0;\n256:     let replayed = 0;\n257:     try {\n258:       const result = await backfillRunLog({\n259:         inPath,\n260:         outPath,\n261:         promptFor: () => 'prompt',\n262:         warm: async () => {\n263:           warmed += 1;\n264:         },\n265:         replay: async () => {\n266:           replayed += 1;\n267:           return { offeredCommand: '/doctor:git hooks', offeredPolicy: 'run', offerError: null };\n268:         },\n269:         brief: async () => 'unknown',\n270:       });\n271: \n272:       expect(result).toMatchObject({ ok: true, lines: 1 });\n273:       const [written] = readFileSync(outPath, 'utf8').trim().split('\\n').map((line) => JSON.parse(line));\n274:       expect(written).toMatchObject({\n275:         offeredCommand: null,\n276:         offeredPolicy: null,\n277:         offerError: 'no_checkout',\n278:         briefDelivered: 'unknown',\n279:         offerBackfilled: true,\n280:       });\n281:       expect(warmed).toBe(0);\n282:       expect(replayed).toBe(0);\n283:     } finally {\n284:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n285:     }\n286:   });\n287: \n288:   it('refuses a row the prompt lookup rejects before it writes anything', async () => {\n289:     const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n290:     const outPath = join(dir, 'runs-out.jsonl');\n291:     const inPath = writeRunLog(dir, [\n292:       { runtime: 'claude', rowId: 'row-plain', arm: 'pre', locator: { projectDir: '/checkout-one' }, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n293:       { runtime: 'claude', rowId: 'row-holdout', arm: 'pre', locator: { projectDir: '/checkout-one' }, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n294:     ]);\n295:     let replayed = 0;\n296:     try {\n297:       const result = await backfillRunLog({\n298:         inPath,\n299:         outPath,\n300:         promptFor: (rowId: string) => {\n301:           if (rowId === 'row-holdout') {\n302:             throw Object.assign(new Error(`${rowId}: a holdout row is never run`), { code: 'holdout_row' });\n303:           }\n304:           return 'prompt';\n305:         },\n306:         replay: async () => {\n307:           replayed += 1;\n308:           return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError: null };\n309:         },\n310:         brief: async () => 'unknown',\n311:       });\n312: \n313:       expect(result).toMatchObject({ ok: false, code: 'holdout_row' });\n314:       expect(existsSync(outPath)).toBe(false);\n315:       expect(replayed).toBe(0);\n316:     } finally {\n317:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n318:     }\n319:   });\n320: \n321:   it('refuses to write over its own input', async () => {\n322:     const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n323:     const inPath = writeRunLog(dir, [\n324:       { runtime: 'claude', rowId: 'row-one', arm: 'pre', locator: { projectDir: '/checkout-one' }, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n325:     ]);\n326:     const before = readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8');\n327:     try {\n328:       const result = await backfillRunLog({ inPath, outPath: inPath, promptFor: () => 'prompt' });\n329: \n330:       expect(result).toMatchObject({ ok: false, code: 'same_path' });\n331:       expect(readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8')).toBe(before);\n332:     } finally {\n333:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n334:     }\n335:   });\n336: });","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-offer.vitest.ts, lines 1-336\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Command offer\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: \n5: // The head is the only advisor text that names both the offered command and the\n6: // policy, so these cases pin each shape a live head takes, a command with a\n7: // policy, a command under an ambiguous head without one, and a head that names\n8: // no command at all. The replay case proves a checkout without the prompt hook\n9: // reports the miss, because a backfill off such a checkout has to stay readable\n10: // instead of throwing away the lines around it. The delivery cases pin the two\n11: // real answers a Claude transcript can give and the third answer a runtime with\n12: // no confirmed delivery point must give, so a later summary never reads a\n13: // missing record as a miss. The backfill cases pin that a line keeps its place\n14: // when its checkout is gone, that a refused row leaves no output behind, and\n15: // that the new log never replaces the one it reads.\n16: \n17: import { existsSync, mkdirSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, realpathSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n18: import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\n19: import { join } from 'node:path';\n20: \n21: import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\n22: \n23: import {\n24:   backfillRunLog,\n25:   briefDelivered,\n26:   codexHookApproval,\n27:   parseOffer,\n28:   replayOffer,\n29: } from '../scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs';\n30: \n31: const OFFERED_HEAD = 'Advisor: live; use sk-git → /doctor:git hooks (ask) 0.82/0.12 pass.';\n32: const AMBIGUOUS_HEAD = 'Advisor: live; ambiguous: /doctor:git hooks 0.82/0.12 vs sk-git 0.86/0.16 pass.';\n33: const NO_COMMAND_HEAD = 'Advisor: live; use command-speckit-plan 0.82/0.16 pass.';\n34: const CODEX_HOOKS = {\n35:   hooks: {\n36:     UserPromptSubmit: [\n37:       { hooks: [{ command: 'node x/hooks/codex/user-prompt-submit.js' }] },\n38:     ],\n39:   },\n40: };\n41: \n42: function writeTranscript(lines: unknown[]): { dir: string; transcriptPath: string } {\n43:   const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n44:   const transcriptPath = join(dir, 'session.jsonl');\n45:   writeFileSync(transcriptPath, lines.map((line) => JSON.stringify(line)).join('\\n'));\n46:   return { dir, transcriptPath };\n47: }\n48: \n49: function writeRunLog(dir: string, lines: unknown[]): string {\n50:   const inPath = join(dir, 'runs.jsonl');\n51:   writeFileSync(inPath, lines.map((line) => JSON.stringify(line)).join('\\n') + '\\n');\n52:   return inPath;\n53: }\n54: \n55: function writeCodexRoot(withHooks: boolean): { dir: string; hooksPath: string } {\n56:   const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-codex-'));\n57:   const hooksPath = join(dir, '.codex', 'hooks.json');\n58:   if (withHooks) {\n59:     mkdirSync(join(dir, '.codex'), { recursive: true });\n60:     writeFileSync(hooksPath, JSON.stringify(CODEX_HOOKS));\n61:   }\n62:   return { dir, hooksPath };\n63: }\n64: \n65: function writeCodexConfig(dir: string, lines: string[]): string {\n66:   const configPath = join(dir, 'config.toml');\n67:   writeFileSync(configPath, lines.map((line) => `${line}\\n`).join(''));\n68:   return configPath;\n69: }\n70: \n71: describe('parseOffer', () => {\n72:   it('reads the command and the policy a head offers', () => {\n73:     expect(parseOffer(OFFERED_HEAD)).toEqual({\n74:       offeredCommand: '/doctor:git hooks',\n75:       offeredPolicy: 'ask',\n76:     });\n77:   });\n78: \n79:   it('reads the command but no policy under an ambiguous head', () => {\n80:     expect(parseOffer(AMBIGUOUS_HEAD)).toEqual({\n81:       offeredCommand: '/doctor:git hooks',\n82:       offeredPolicy: null,\n83:     });\n84:   });\n85: \n86:   it('reads no command when the head names none', () => {\n87:     expect(parseOffer(NO_COMMAND_HEAD)).toEqual({\n88:       offeredCommand: null,\n89:       offeredPolicy: null,\n90:     });\n91:   });\n92: });\n93: \n94: describe('replayOffer', () => {\n95:   it('reports a checkout without the prompt hook instead of throwing', () => {\n96:     const checkoutDir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n97:     try {\n98:       expect(replayOffer({ checkoutDir, prompt: 'create a readme', sessionId: 'offer-test' })).toEqual({\n99:         offeredCommand: null,\n100:         offeredPolicy: null,\n101:         offerError: 'hook_missing',\n102:       });\n103:     } finally {\n104:       rmSync(checkoutDir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n105:     }\n106:   });\n107: });\n108: \n109: describe('codexHookApproval', () => {\n110:   it('reports an advisor hook that no config entry approves as unapproved', () => {\n111:     const { dir } = writeCodexRoot(true);\n112:     try {\n113:       const configPath = writeCodexConfig(dir, []);\n114:       expect(codexHookApproval({ projectDir: dir, configPath })).toBe('unapproved');\n115:     } finally {\n116:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n117:     }\n118:   });\n119: \n120:   it('reports an advisor hook approved by a trusted hash as approved', () => {\n121:     const { dir, hooksPath } = writeCodexRoot(true);\n122:     try {\n123:       const configPath = writeCodexConfig(dir, [\n124:         `[hooks.state.\"${realpathSync(hooksPath)}:user_prompt_submit:0:0\"]`,\n125:         'trusted_hash = \"x\"',\n126:       ]);\n127:       expect(codexHookApproval({ projectDir: dir, configPath })).toBe('approved');\n128:     } finally {\n129:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n130:     }\n131:   });\n132: \n133:   it('reports a root with no hooks file as no-hooks', () => {\n134:     const { dir } = writeCodexRoot(false);\n135:     try {\n136:       expect(codexHookApproval({ projectDir: dir })).toBe('no-hooks');\n137:     } finally {\n138:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n139:     }\n140:   });\n141: });\n142: \n143: describe('briefDelivered', () => {\n144:   it('finds the advisor context in a Claude transcript attachment', async () => {\n145:     const { dir, transcriptPath } = writeTranscript([\n146:       { type: 'user', message: { role: 'user', content: 'create a readme' } },\n147:       {\n148:         type: 'attachment',\n149:         attachment: {\n150:           type: 'hook_additional_context',\n151:           hookEvent: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n152:           content: [OFFERED_HEAD],\n153:         },\n154:       },\n155:     ]);\n156:     try {\n157:       await expect(briefDelivered({ runtime: 'claude', locator: { transcriptPath } })).resolves.toBe(true);\n158:     } finally {\n159:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n160:     }\n161:   });\n162: \n163:   it('returns false when the Claude transcript holds no advisor attachment', async () => {\n164:     const { dir, transcriptPath } = writeTranscript([\n165:       { type: 'user', message: { role: 'user', content: 'create a readme' } },\n166:       { type: 'attachment', attachment: { type: 'hook_additional_context', hookEvent: 'SessionStart', content: ['Session ready'] } },\n167:     ]);\n168:     try {\n169:       await expect(briefDelivered({ runtime: 'claude', locator: { transcriptPath } })).resolves.toBe(false);\n170:     } finally {\n171:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n172:     }\n173:   });\n174: \n175:   it('returns unknown for a runtime with no confirmed delivery point', async () => {\n176:     await expect(briefDelivered({\n177:       runtime: 'devin',\n178:       locator: { transcriptPath: join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-devin.jsonl') },\n179:     })).resolves.toBe('unknown');\n180:   });\n181: \n182:   it('returns false for a Codex hook the config never approved', async () => {\n183:     const { dir } = writeCodexRoot(true);\n184:     try {\n185:       const configPath = writeCodexConfig(dir, []);\n186:       await expect(briefDelivered({\n187:         runtime: 'codex',\n188:         locator: { projectDir: dir },\n189:         codexConfig: configPath,\n190:       })).resolves.toBe(false);\n191:     } finally {\n192:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n193:     }\n194:   });\n195: });\n196: \n197: describe('backfillRunLog', () => {\n198:   it('adds the offer and the brief to every line and warms each checkout once', async () => {\n199:     const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n200:     const outPath = join(dir, 'runs-out.jsonl');\n201:     const inPath = writeRunLog(dir, [\n202:       { runtime: 'claude', rowId: 'row-one', arm: 'pre', locator: { projectDir: '/checkout-one' }, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n203:       { runtime: 'claude', rowId: 'row-two', arm: 'pre', locator: { projectDir: '/checkout-one' }, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n204:     ]);\n205:     const before = readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8');\n206:     const warmed: string[] = [];\n207:     const replayed: Array<{ checkoutDir: string; prompt: string; sessionId: string }> = [];\n208:     const seen: Array<{ runtime: string; locator: unknown }> = [];\n209:     try {\n210:       const result = await backfillRunLog({\n211:         inPath,\n212:         outPath,\n213:         promptFor: (rowId: string) => `prompt for ${rowId}`,\n214:         warm: async (checkoutDir: string) => {\n215:           warmed.push(checkoutDir);\n216:         },\n217:         replay: async ({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId }: { checkoutDir: string; prompt: string; sessionId: string }) => {\n218:           replayed.push({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId });\n219:           return { offeredCommand: '/doctor:git hooks', offeredPolicy: 'ask', offerError: null };\n220:         },\n221:         brief: async ({ runtime, locator }: { runtime: string; locator: unknown }) => {\n222:           seen.push({ runtime, locator });\n223:           return true;\n224:         },\n225:       });\n226: \n227:       expect(result).toEqual({ ok: true, lines: 2, outPath });\n228:       const written = readFileSync(outPath, 'utf8').trim().split('\\n').map((line) => JSON.parse(line));\n229:       expect(written).toHaveLength(2);\n230:       expect(written[0]).toMatchObject({\n231:         rowId: 'row-one',\n232:         offeredCommand: '/doctor:git hooks',\n233:         offeredPolicy: 'ask',\n234:         offerError: null,\n235:         briefDelivered: true,\n236:         offerBackfilled: true,\n237:       });\n238:       expect(written[1].rowId).toBe('row-two');\n239:       expect(warmed).toEqual(['/checkout-one']);\n240:       expect(replayed.map((call) => call.sessionId)).toEqual(['backfill-row-one', 'backfill-row-two']);\n241:       expect(replayed[0]).toMatchObject({ checkoutDir: '/checkout-one', prompt: 'prompt for row-one' });\n242:       expect(seen).toHaveLength(2);\n243:       expect(readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8')).toBe(before);\n244:     } finally {\n245:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n246:     }\n247:   });\n248: \n249:   it('marks a line whose locator names no checkout instead of replaying it', async () => {\n250:     const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n251:     const outPath = join(dir, 'runs-out.jsonl');\n252:     const inPath = writeRunLog(dir, [\n253:       { runtime: 'codex', rowId: 'row-no-checkout', arm: 'pre', locator: null, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n254:     ]);\n255:     let warmed = 0;\n256:     let replayed = 0;\n257:     try {\n258:       const result = await backfillRunLog({\n259:         inPath,\n260:         outPath,\n261:         promptFor: () => 'prompt',\n262:         warm: async () => {\n263:           warmed += 1;\n264:         },\n265:         replay: async () => {\n266:           replayed += 1;\n267:           return { offeredCommand: '/doctor:git hooks', offeredPolicy: 'run', offerError: null };\n268:         },\n269:         brief: async () => 'unknown',\n270:       });\n271: \n272:       expect(result).toMatchObject({ ok: true, lines: 1 });\n273:       const [written] = readFileSync(outPath, 'utf8').trim().split('\\n').map((line) => JSON.parse(line));\n274:       expect(written).toMatchObject({\n275:         offeredCommand: null,\n276:         offeredPolicy: null,\n277:         offerError: 'no_checkout',\n278:         briefDelivered: 'unknown',\n279:         offerBackfilled: true,\n280:       });\n281:       expect(warmed).toBe(0);\n282:       expect(replayed).toBe(0);\n283:     } finally {\n284:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n285:     }\n286:   });\n287: \n288:   it('refuses a row the prompt lookup rejects before it writes anything', async () => {\n289:     const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n290:     const outPath = join(dir, 'runs-out.jsonl');\n291:     const inPath = writeRunLog(dir, [\n292:       { runtime: 'claude', rowId: 'row-plain', arm: 'pre', locator: { projectDir: '/checkout-one' }, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n293:       { runtime: 'claude', rowId: 'row-holdout', arm: 'pre', locator: { projectDir: '/checkout-one' }, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n294:     ]);\n295:     let replayed = 0;\n296:     try {\n297:       const result = await backfillRunLog({\n298:         inPath,\n299:         outPath,\n300:         promptFor: (rowId: string) => {\n301:           if (rowId === 'row-holdout') {\n302:             throw Object.assign(new Error(`${rowId}: a holdout row is never run`), { code: 'holdout_row' });\n303:           }\n304:           return 'prompt';\n305:         },\n306:         replay: async () => {\n307:           replayed += 1;\n308:           return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError: null };\n309:         },\n310:         brief: async () => 'unknown',\n311:       });\n312: \n313:       expect(result).toMatchObject({ ok: false, code: 'holdout_row' });\n314:       expect(existsSync(outPath)).toBe(false);\n315:       expect(replayed).toBe(0);\n316:     } finally {\n317:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n318:     }\n319:   });\n320: \n321:   it('refuses to write over its own input', async () => {\n322:     const dir = mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'command-offer-'));\n323:     const inPath = writeRunLog(dir, [\n324:       { runtime: 'claude', rowId: 'row-one', arm: 'pre', locator: { projectDir: '/checkout-one' }, exit: 0, changedPaths: [] },\n325:     ]);\n326:     const before = readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8');\n327:     try {\n328:       const result = await backfillRunLog({ inPath, outPath: inPath, promptFor: () => 'prompt' });\n329: \n330:       expect(result).toMatchObject({ ok: false, code: 'same_path' });\n331:       expect(readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8')).toBe(before);\n332:     } finally {\n333:       rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n334:     }\n335:   });\n336: });"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/tests/command-offer.vitest.ts\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546178659,"end":1794546179031}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546179037,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","part":{"partID":"prt_1d34998d2001WvoKOOFPZZmZSP","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","messageID":"msg_1d34952d6001WWg3jQehSp3lgy","type":"tool","id":"call_6623cdada5fe49cc8d49a64f","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs, lines 1-394\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Command offer from an advisor head\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // A run line records which command the advisor offered and the policy it named,\n6: // so a start rate can be read as \"did the reader follow the offer\" rather than\n7: // only \"did a command run\". The offer is recovered by replaying the Claude\n8: // prompt hook after the turn, because the head is the only text that names both\n9: // the command and the policy, and a replay keeps the recorded value independent\n10: // of whatever the session reader happened to retain.\n11: //\n12: // A failed replay returns null fields plus an offerError, so a backfill over a\n13: // checkout without the hook, or a hook that crashes, still produces a readable\n14: // line instead of aborting the run.\n15: //\n16: // The offer alone cannot tell a reader who ignored it from a brief the session\n17: // never received, so the same module also reports whether the brief landed. A\n18: // runtime with no confirmed place to look reports `unknown` rather than a miss,\n19: // because a missing record there measures the reader, not the delivery.\n20: //\n21: // A log written before those fields existed gets them from a backfill, which\n22: // resolves every prompt before it writes, so a refused row leaves no partial log\n23: // behind and each line still answers for its own checkout.\n24: \n25: import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n26: import { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n27: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n28: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n29: \n30: import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n31: \n32: const HOOK_RELATIVE = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js';\n33: const ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE = '.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs';\n34: const OFFER_PATTERN = /\\/[a-z][a-z-]*:[a-z][a-z-]*(?: [a-z][a-z-]*)?/;\n35: const POLICY_PATTERN = /^ *(?:\\((run|start|ask)\\))/;\n36: const BRIEF_PATTERN = /Advisor: [a-z]+[;. ]/;\n37: const CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT = 'UserPromptSubmit';\n38: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;\n39: const MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES = 1024 * 1024;\n40: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '../../../../../..');\n41: const CODEX_CHECK = join(REPO_ROOT, '.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs');\n42: \n43: function failedOffer(offerError) {\n44:   return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError };\n45: }\n46: \n47: /**\n48:  * Read the command and the policy out of the first line of a head.\n49:  *\n50:  * The policy only counts when it directly follows the command, so a `(ask)`\n51:  * that belongs to a later clause cannot attach itself to an earlier match.\n52:  *\n53:  * @param {string} head - One line of advisor output.\n54:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null }}\n55:  */\n56: export function parseOffer(head) {\n57:   const text = typeof head === 'string' ? head : '';\n58:   const match = text.match(OFFER_PATTERN);\n59:   if (!match) {\n60:     return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null };\n61:   }\n62:   const policy = text.slice(match.index + match[0].length).match(POLICY_PATTERN);\n63:   return {\n64:     offeredCommand: match[0],\n65:     offeredPolicy: policy ? policy[1] : null,\n66:   };\n67: }\n68: \n69: /**\n70:  * Replay the Claude prompt hook for one prompt and parse the offer it prints.\n71:  *\n72:  * The hook is resolved from the checkout rather than from this module, so a\n73:  * backfill records what that checkout's advisor would have offered at the time\n74:  * of the run, not what the current working copy happens to say.\n75:  *\n76:  * @param {object} options\n77:  * @param {string} options.checkoutDir - Root of the checkout whose hook runs.\n78:  * @param {string} options.prompt - The prompt to replay.\n79:  * @param {string} options.sessionId - Session id for the hook payload.\n80:  * @param {number} [options.timeoutMs] - Hook timeout before the replay fails.\n81:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null, offerError: string | null }}\n82:  */\n83: export function replayOffer({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId, timeoutMs = DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS }) {\n84:   const hookPath = join(checkoutDir, HOOK_RELATIVE);\n85:   if (!existsSync(hookPath)) {\n86:     return failedOffer('hook_missing');\n87:   }\n88: \n89:   const payload = {\n90:     prompt,\n91:     cwd: checkoutDir,\n92:     hook_event_name: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n93:     session_id: sessionId,\n94:   };\n95: \n96:   let result;\n97:   try {\n98:     result = spawnSync('node', [hookPath], {\n99:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n100:       input: JSON.stringify(payload),\n101:       encoding: 'utf8',\n102:       timeout: timeoutMs,\n103:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n104:     });\n105:   } catch {\n106:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n107:   }\n108: \n109:   if (result.error || result.status !== 0 || typeof result.stdout !== 'string') {\n110:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n111:   }\n112: \n113:   let output;\n114:   try {\n115:     output = JSON.parse(result.stdout);\n116:   } catch {\n117:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n118:   }\n119: \n120:   const context = output?.hookSpecificOutput?.additionalContext;\n121:   if (typeof context !== 'string') {\n122:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n123:   }\n124: \n125:   const [firstLine = ''] = context.split(/\\r?\\n/);\n126:   if (!firstLine.trim()) {\n127:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n128:   }\n129: \n130:   return { ...parseOffer(firstLine), offerError: null };\n131: }\n132: \n133: function contentHasBrief(content) {\n134:   if (typeof content === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(content);\n135:   if (!Array.isArray(content)) return false;\n136:   return content.some((part) => {\n137:     if (typeof part === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part);\n138:     return typeof part?.text === 'string' && BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part.text);\n139:   });\n140: }\n141: \n142: // Neutral records keep their text in content blocks, and the block shape differs\n143: // by runtime, so the serialized record is the one place the brief is found\n144: // whichever block carries it.\n145: function recordsHaveBrief(records) {\n146:   return (Array.isArray(records) ? records : []).some((record) =>\n147:     BRIEF_PATTERN.test(JSON.stringify(record ?? null)));\n148: }\n149: \n150: /**\n151:  * Ask the project's Codex approval check whether its advisor hook can run.\n152:  *\n153:  * Codex skips an unapproved project hook without a warning, so a wired hook can\n154:  * still deliver nothing. The check reports the approval recorded in the config\n155:  * file it reads, and this wrapper turns that verdict into the answer the\n156:  * delivery decision needs.\n157:  *\n158:  * @param {object} [options]\n159:  * @param {string} [options.projectDir] - Project root whose hooks file is read.\n160:  * @param {string} [options.configPath] - Codex config file, defaulting to the\n161:  *   environment's Codex home.\n162:  * @returns {'approved' | 'unapproved' | 'absent' | 'no-hooks' | 'unknown'}\n163:  */\n164: export function codexHookApproval({ projectDir, configPath } = {}) {\n165:   if (typeof projectDir !== 'string' || projectDir === '') return 'unknown';\n166: \n167:   const args = [CODEX_CHECK, '--root', projectDir, '--json'];\n168:   if (configPath) args.push('--config', configPath);\n169:   const result = spawnSync('node', args, { encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 10_000 });\n170:   if (result.error || typeof result.stdout !== 'string') return 'unknown';\n171: \n172:   let verdict;\n173:   try {\n174:     verdict = JSON.parse(result.stdout);\n175:   } catch {\n176:     return 'unknown';\n177:   }\n178: \n179:   switch (verdict?.code) {\n180:     case 'codex-hooks-approved':\n181:       return 'approved';\n182:     case 'codex-hooks-unapproved':\n183:       return 'unapproved';\n184:     case 'codex-advisor-hook-absent':\n185:       return 'absent';\n186:     case 'no-codex-hooks':\n187:       return 'no-hooks';\n188:     default:\n189:       return 'unknown';\n190:   }\n191: }\n192: \n193: /**\n194:  * Decide whether the advisor brief reached the session that ran the turn.\n195:  *\n196:  * A Claude transcript records the hook context as its own attachment line, so a\n197:  * missing attachment is a real miss. Pi keeps delivery in its session records\n198:  * and needs the caller's reader. Every other runtime has no confirmed record of\n199:  * delivery, so its answer stays `unknown` and never counts as a miss.\n200:  *\n201:  * @param {object} options\n202:  * @param {string} options.runtime - The measured runtime.\n203:  * @param {object|null} [options.locator] - The locator the run line recorded.\n204:  * @param {Function} [options.loadSession] - Reader for a runtime whose brief\n205:  *   lives in session records rather than a transcript file.\n206:  * @param {string} [options.codexConfig] - Codex config file the approval is\n207:  *   read from, defaulting to the environment's Codex home.\n208:  * @returns {Promise<boolean | 'unknown'>}\n209:  */\n210: export async function briefDelivered({ runtime, locator, loadSession, codexConfig } = {}) {\n211:   if (runtime === 'claude') {\n212:     const transcriptPath = locator?.transcriptPath;\n213:     if (typeof transcriptPath !== 'string' || transcriptPath.length === 0) {\n214:       return 'unknown';\n215:     }\n216: \n217:     let raw;\n218:     try {\n219:       raw = readFileSync(transcriptPath, 'utf8');\n220:     } catch {\n221:       return 'unknown';\n222:     }\n223: \n224:     for (const line of raw.split('\\n')) {\n225:       if (!line.trim()) continue;\n226:       let entry;\n227:       try {\n228:         entry = JSON.parse(line);\n229:       } catch {\n230:         continue;\n231:       }\n232:       if (entry?.type !== 'attachment') continue;\n233:       const attachment = entry.attachment;\n234:       if (attachment?.type !== 'hook_additional_context') continue;\n235:       if (attachment.hookEvent !== CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT) continue;\n236:       if (contentHasBrief(attachment.content)) return true;\n237:     }\n238:     return false;\n239:   }\n240: \n241:   if (runtime === 'pi') {\n242:     if (typeof loadSession !== 'function') return 'unknown';\n243:     let loaded;\n244:     try {\n245:       loaded = await loadSession({ ...locator, runtime });\n246:     } catch {\n247:       return 'unknown';\n248:     }\n249:     if (!loaded?.ok) return 'unknown';\n250:     return recordsHaveBrief(loaded.records);\n251:   }\n252: \n253:   if (runtime === 'codex') {\n254:     // An unapproved project hook never runs, so the brief cannot have arrived.\n255:     // An approved one may store its context where no reader looks yet.\n256:     if (codexHookApproval({ projectDir: locator?.projectDir, configPath: codexConfig }) === 'unapproved') {\n257:       return false;\n258:     }\n259: \n260:     if (typeof loadSession !== 'function') return 'unknown';\n261:     let loaded;\n262:     try {\n263:       loaded = await loadSession({ ...locator, runtime });\n264:     } catch {\n265:       return 'unknown';\n266:     }\n267:     if (!loaded?.ok) return 'unknown';\n268:     return recordsHaveBrief(loaded.records) ? true : 'unknown';\n269:   }\n270: \n271:   return 'unknown';\n272: }\n273: \n274: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n275: // Run-log backfill\n276: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n277: \n278: // A fresh daemon can outlast the first prompt's budget, so the backfill asks\n279: // each checkout to start one before it replays. The status call writes nothing,\n280: // and its failure is ignored because a replay that finds no daemon records its\n281: // own error instead of losing the line.\n282: function warmCheckout(checkoutDir) {\n283:   const cliPath = join(checkoutDir, ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE);\n284:   if (!existsSync(cliPath)) return;\n285:   spawnSync(\n286:     'node',\n287:     [cliPath, 'advisor_status', '--workspace-root', checkoutDir, '--format', 'json', '--no-warm-only'],\n288:     {\n289:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n290:       encoding: 'utf8',\n291:       timeout: DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS,\n292:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n293:     },\n294:   );\n295: }\n296: \n297: function refused(code, detail) {\n298:   return { ok: false, code, detail };\n299: }\n300: \n301: /**\n302:  * Add the offer, its policy and the brief to every line of a run log.\n303:  *\n304:  * The prompts are resolved before the first line is written, so a row the\n305:  * corpus refuses leaves no output file behind. A line whose locator names no\n306:  * checkout keeps null offer fields and its own error code, and the rest of the\n307:  * log is still written.\n308:  *\n309:  * @param {object} options\n310:  * @param {string} options.inPath - Run log to read.\n311:  * @param {string} options.outPath - New run log to write.\n312:  * @param {(rowId: string) => string} options.promptFor - Prompt lookup. It\n313:  *   refuses an unknown or holdout row by throwing an error with a `code`.\n314:  * @param {Function} [options.replay] - Offer replay, injectable for tests.\n315:  * @param {Function} [options.brief] - Brief delivery reader, injectable for tests.\n316:  * @param {(checkoutDir: string) => unknown} [options.warm] - Daemon warm-up,\n317:  *   injectable for tests.\n318:  * @returns {Promise<{ ok: true, lines: number, outPath: string }\n319:  *   | { ok: false, code: string, detail: string }>}\n320:  */\n321: export async function backfillRunLog({\n322:   inPath,\n323:   outPath,\n324:   promptFor,\n325:   replay = replayOffer,\n326:   brief = briefDelivered,\n327:   warm = warmCheckout,\n328: }) {\n329:   if (resolve(outPath) === resolve(inPath)) {\n330:     return refused('same_path', `${outPath}: the backfill writes a new log`);\n331:   }\n332: \n333:   const rows = [];\n334:   for (const raw of readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n335:     if (raw.trim() === '') continue;\n336:     rows.push(JSON.parse(raw));\n337:   }\n338: \n339:   const prompts = [];\n340:   for (const row of rows) {\n341:     try {\n342:       prompts.push(promptFor(row.rowId));\n343:     } catch (error) {\n344:       return refused(error?.code ?? 'unknown_row', error?.message ?? String(error));\n345:     }\n346:   }\n347: \n348:   const warmed = new Set();\n349:   const output = [];\n350:   for (let index = 0; index < rows.length; index += 1) {\n351:     const line = rows[index];\n352:     const rowId = line.rowId;\n353:     const locator = line.locator !== null && typeof line.locator === 'object' && !Array.isArray(line.locator)\n354:       ? line.locator\n355:       : null;\n356:     const projectDir = locator?.projectDir;\n357:     const checkoutDir = typeof projectDir === 'string' && projectDir !== '' ? projectDir : null;\n358: \n359:     let offer = { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError: 'no_checkout' };\n360:     if (checkoutDir !== null) {\n361:       if (!warmed.has(checkoutDir)) {\n362:         warmed.add(checkoutDir);\n363:         try {\n364:           await warm(checkoutDir);\n365:         } catch {\n366:           // A failed warm-up still lets the replay report its own miss.\n367:         }\n368:       }\n369:       offer = await replay({\n370:         checkoutDir,\n371:         prompt: prompts[index],\n372:         sessionId: `backfill-${rowId}`,\n373:       });\n374:     }\n375: \n376:     // Pi keeps its brief in a session store rather than a transcript, so the\n377:     // shared reader travels with every call. A runtime that has no reader for\n378:     // its store ignores it and answers `unknown`.\n379:     const delivered = await brief({ runtime: line.runtime, locator, loadSession: loadSharedSession });\n380: \n381:     output.push({\n382:       ...line,\n383:       offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand ?? null,\n384:       offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy ?? null,\n385:       offerError: offer.offerError ?? null,\n386:       briefDelivered: delivered,\n387:       offerBackfilled: true,\n388:     });\n389:   }\n390: \n391:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n392:   writeFileSync(outPath, output.map((line) => `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`).join(''), 'utf8');\n393:   return { ok: true, lines: output.length, outPath };\n394: }","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs, lines 1-394\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: Command offer from an advisor head\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // A run line records which command the advisor offered and the policy it named,\n6: // so a start rate can be read as \"did the reader follow the offer\" rather than\n7: // only \"did a command run\". The offer is recovered by replaying the Claude\n8: // prompt hook after the turn, because the head is the only text that names both\n9: // the command and the policy, and a replay keeps the recorded value independent\n10: // of whatever the session reader happened to retain.\n11: //\n12: // A failed replay returns null fields plus an offerError, so a backfill over a\n13: // checkout without the hook, or a hook that crashes, still produces a readable\n14: // line instead of aborting the run.\n15: //\n16: // The offer alone cannot tell a reader who ignored it from a brief the session\n17: // never received, so the same module also reports whether the brief landed. A\n18: // runtime with no confirmed place to look reports `unknown` rather than a miss,\n19: // because a missing record there measures the reader, not the delivery.\n20: //\n21: // A log written before those fields existed gets them from a backfill, which\n22: // resolves every prompt before it writes, so a refused row leaves no partial log\n23: // behind and each line still answers for its own checkout.\n24: \n25: import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n26: import { existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n27: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n28: import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n29: \n30: import { loadSession as loadSharedSession } from '../../../../../hooks/shared/session-readers/index.mjs';\n31: \n32: const HOOK_RELATIVE = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/dist/hooks/claude/user-prompt-submit.js';\n33: const ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE = '.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs';\n34: const OFFER_PATTERN = /\\/[a-z][a-z-]*:[a-z][a-z-]*(?: [a-z][a-z-]*)?/;\n35: const POLICY_PATTERN = /^ *(?:\\((run|start|ask)\\))/;\n36: const BRIEF_PATTERN = /Advisor: [a-z]+[;. ]/;\n37: const CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT = 'UserPromptSubmit';\n38: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS = 30_000;\n39: const MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES = 1024 * 1024;\n40: const REPO_ROOT = resolve(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '../../../../../..');\n41: const CODEX_CHECK = join(REPO_ROOT, '.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/codex-hook-approval-check.cjs');\n42: \n43: function failedOffer(offerError) {\n44:   return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError };\n45: }\n46: \n47: /**\n48:  * Read the command and the policy out of the first line of a head.\n49:  *\n50:  * The policy only counts when it directly follows the command, so a `(ask)`\n51:  * that belongs to a later clause cannot attach itself to an earlier match.\n52:  *\n53:  * @param {string} head - One line of advisor output.\n54:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null }}\n55:  */\n56: export function parseOffer(head) {\n57:   const text = typeof head === 'string' ? head : '';\n58:   const match = text.match(OFFER_PATTERN);\n59:   if (!match) {\n60:     return { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null };\n61:   }\n62:   const policy = text.slice(match.index + match[0].length).match(POLICY_PATTERN);\n63:   return {\n64:     offeredCommand: match[0],\n65:     offeredPolicy: policy ? policy[1] : null,\n66:   };\n67: }\n68: \n69: /**\n70:  * Replay the Claude prompt hook for one prompt and parse the offer it prints.\n71:  *\n72:  * The hook is resolved from the checkout rather than from this module, so a\n73:  * backfill records what that checkout's advisor would have offered at the time\n74:  * of the run, not what the current working copy happens to say.\n75:  *\n76:  * @param {object} options\n77:  * @param {string} options.checkoutDir - Root of the checkout whose hook runs.\n78:  * @param {string} options.prompt - The prompt to replay.\n79:  * @param {string} options.sessionId - Session id for the hook payload.\n80:  * @param {number} [options.timeoutMs] - Hook timeout before the replay fails.\n81:  * @returns {{ offeredCommand: string | null, offeredPolicy: string | null, offerError: string | null }}\n82:  */\n83: export function replayOffer({ checkoutDir, prompt, sessionId, timeoutMs = DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS }) {\n84:   const hookPath = join(checkoutDir, HOOK_RELATIVE);\n85:   if (!existsSync(hookPath)) {\n86:     return failedOffer('hook_missing');\n87:   }\n88: \n89:   const payload = {\n90:     prompt,\n91:     cwd: checkoutDir,\n92:     hook_event_name: 'UserPromptSubmit',\n93:     session_id: sessionId,\n94:   };\n95: \n96:   let result;\n97:   try {\n98:     result = spawnSync('node', [hookPath], {\n99:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n100:       input: JSON.stringify(payload),\n101:       encoding: 'utf8',\n102:       timeout: timeoutMs,\n103:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n104:     });\n105:   } catch {\n106:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n107:   }\n108: \n109:   if (result.error || result.status !== 0 || typeof result.stdout !== 'string') {\n110:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n111:   }\n112: \n113:   let output;\n114:   try {\n115:     output = JSON.parse(result.stdout);\n116:   } catch {\n117:     return failedOffer('hook_failed');\n118:   }\n119: \n120:   const context = output?.hookSpecificOutput?.additionalContext;\n121:   if (typeof context !== 'string') {\n122:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n123:   }\n124: \n125:   const [firstLine = ''] = context.split(/\\r?\\n/);\n126:   if (!firstLine.trim()) {\n127:     return failedOffer('no_head');\n128:   }\n129: \n130:   return { ...parseOffer(firstLine), offerError: null };\n131: }\n132: \n133: function contentHasBrief(content) {\n134:   if (typeof content === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(content);\n135:   if (!Array.isArray(content)) return false;\n136:   return content.some((part) => {\n137:     if (typeof part === 'string') return BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part);\n138:     return typeof part?.text === 'string' && BRIEF_PATTERN.test(part.text);\n139:   });\n140: }\n141: \n142: // Neutral records keep their text in content blocks, and the block shape differs\n143: // by runtime, so the serialized record is the one place the brief is found\n144: // whichever block carries it.\n145: function recordsHaveBrief(records) {\n146:   return (Array.isArray(records) ? records : []).some((record) =>\n147:     BRIEF_PATTERN.test(JSON.stringify(record ?? null)));\n148: }\n149: \n150: /**\n151:  * Ask the project's Codex approval check whether its advisor hook can run.\n152:  *\n153:  * Codex skips an unapproved project hook without a warning, so a wired hook can\n154:  * still deliver nothing. The check reports the approval recorded in the config\n155:  * file it reads, and this wrapper turns that verdict into the answer the\n156:  * delivery decision needs.\n157:  *\n158:  * @param {object} [options]\n159:  * @param {string} [options.projectDir] - Project root whose hooks file is read.\n160:  * @param {string} [options.configPath] - Codex config file, defaulting to the\n161:  *   environment's Codex home.\n162:  * @returns {'approved' | 'unapproved' | 'absent' | 'no-hooks' | 'unknown'}\n163:  */\n164: export function codexHookApproval({ projectDir, configPath } = {}) {\n165:   if (typeof projectDir !== 'string' || projectDir === '') return 'unknown';\n166: \n167:   const args = [CODEX_CHECK, '--root', projectDir, '--json'];\n168:   if (configPath) args.push('--config', configPath);\n169:   const result = spawnSync('node', args, { encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 10_000 });\n170:   if (result.error || typeof result.stdout !== 'string') return 'unknown';\n171: \n172:   let verdict;\n173:   try {\n174:     verdict = JSON.parse(result.stdout);\n175:   } catch {\n176:     return 'unknown';\n177:   }\n178: \n179:   switch (verdict?.code) {\n180:     case 'codex-hooks-approved':\n181:       return 'approved';\n182:     case 'codex-hooks-unapproved':\n183:       return 'unapproved';\n184:     case 'codex-advisor-hook-absent':\n185:       return 'absent';\n186:     case 'no-codex-hooks':\n187:       return 'no-hooks';\n188:     default:\n189:       return 'unknown';\n190:   }\n191: }\n192: \n193: /**\n194:  * Decide whether the advisor brief reached the session that ran the turn.\n195:  *\n196:  * A Claude transcript records the hook context as its own attachment line, so a\n197:  * missing attachment is a real miss. Pi keeps delivery in its session records\n198:  * and needs the caller's reader. Every other runtime has no confirmed record of\n199:  * delivery, so its answer stays `unknown` and never counts as a miss.\n200:  *\n201:  * @param {object} options\n202:  * @param {string} options.runtime - The measured runtime.\n203:  * @param {object|null} [options.locator] - The locator the run line recorded.\n204:  * @param {Function} [options.loadSession] - Reader for a runtime whose brief\n205:  *   lives in session records rather than a transcript file.\n206:  * @param {string} [options.codexConfig] - Codex config file the approval is\n207:  *   read from, defaulting to the environment's Codex home.\n208:  * @returns {Promise<boolean | 'unknown'>}\n209:  */\n210: export async function briefDelivered({ runtime, locator, loadSession, codexConfig } = {}) {\n211:   if (runtime === 'claude') {\n212:     const transcriptPath = locator?.transcriptPath;\n213:     if (typeof transcriptPath !== 'string' || transcriptPath.length === 0) {\n214:       return 'unknown';\n215:     }\n216: \n217:     let raw;\n218:     try {\n219:       raw = readFileSync(transcriptPath, 'utf8');\n220:     } catch {\n221:       return 'unknown';\n222:     }\n223: \n224:     for (const line of raw.split('\\n')) {\n225:       if (!line.trim()) continue;\n226:       let entry;\n227:       try {\n228:         entry = JSON.parse(line);\n229:       } catch {\n230:         continue;\n231:       }\n232:       if (entry?.type !== 'attachment') continue;\n233:       const attachment = entry.attachment;\n234:       if (attachment?.type !== 'hook_additional_context') continue;\n235:       if (attachment.hookEvent !== CLAUDE_ATTACHMENT_EVENT) continue;\n236:       if (contentHasBrief(attachment.content)) return true;\n237:     }\n238:     return false;\n239:   }\n240: \n241:   if (runtime === 'pi') {\n242:     if (typeof loadSession !== 'function') return 'unknown';\n243:     let loaded;\n244:     try {\n245:       loaded = await loadSession({ ...locator, runtime });\n246:     } catch {\n247:       return 'unknown';\n248:     }\n249:     if (!loaded?.ok) return 'unknown';\n250:     return recordsHaveBrief(loaded.records);\n251:   }\n252: \n253:   if (runtime === 'codex') {\n254:     // An unapproved project hook never runs, so the brief cannot have arrived.\n255:     // An approved one may store its context where no reader looks yet.\n256:     if (codexHookApproval({ projectDir: locator?.projectDir, configPath: codexConfig }) === 'unapproved') {\n257:       return false;\n258:     }\n259: \n260:     if (typeof loadSession !== 'function') return 'unknown';\n261:     let loaded;\n262:     try {\n263:       loaded = await loadSession({ ...locator, runtime });\n264:     } catch {\n265:       return 'unknown';\n266:     }\n267:     if (!loaded?.ok) return 'unknown';\n268:     return recordsHaveBrief(loaded.records) ? true : 'unknown';\n269:   }\n270: \n271:   return 'unknown';\n272: }\n273: \n274: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n275: // Run-log backfill\n276: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n277: \n278: // A fresh daemon can outlast the first prompt's budget, so the backfill asks\n279: // each checkout to start one before it replays. The status call writes nothing,\n280: // and its failure is ignored because a replay that finds no daemon records its\n281: // own error instead of losing the line.\n282: function warmCheckout(checkoutDir) {\n283:   const cliPath = join(checkoutDir, ADVISOR_CLI_RELATIVE);\n284:   if (!existsSync(cliPath)) return;\n285:   spawnSync(\n286:     'node',\n287:     [cliPath, 'advisor_status', '--workspace-root', checkoutDir, '--format', 'json', '--no-warm-only'],\n288:     {\n289:       cwd: checkoutDir,\n290:       encoding: 'utf8',\n291:       timeout: DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS,\n292:       maxBuffer: MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES,\n293:     },\n294:   );\n295: }\n296: \n297: function refused(code, detail) {\n298:   return { ok: false, code, detail };\n299: }\n300: \n301: /**\n302:  * Add the offer, its policy and the brief to every line of a run log.\n303:  *\n304:  * The prompts are resolved before the first line is written, so a row the\n305:  * corpus refuses leaves no output file behind. A line whose locator names no\n306:  * checkout keeps null offer fields and its own error code, and the rest of the\n307:  * log is still written.\n308:  *\n309:  * @param {object} options\n310:  * @param {string} options.inPath - Run log to read.\n311:  * @param {string} options.outPath - New run log to write.\n312:  * @param {(rowId: string) => string} options.promptFor - Prompt lookup. It\n313:  *   refuses an unknown or holdout row by throwing an error with a `code`.\n314:  * @param {Function} [options.replay] - Offer replay, injectable for tests.\n315:  * @param {Function} [options.brief] - Brief delivery reader, injectable for tests.\n316:  * @param {(checkoutDir: string) => unknown} [options.warm] - Daemon warm-up,\n317:  *   injectable for tests.\n318:  * @returns {Promise<{ ok: true, lines: number, outPath: string }\n319:  *   | { ok: false, code: string, detail: string }>}\n320:  */\n321: export async function backfillRunLog({\n322:   inPath,\n323:   outPath,\n324:   promptFor,\n325:   replay = replayOffer,\n326:   brief = briefDelivered,\n327:   warm = warmCheckout,\n328: }) {\n329:   if (resolve(outPath) === resolve(inPath)) {\n330:     return refused('same_path', `${outPath}: the backfill writes a new log`);\n331:   }\n332: \n333:   const rows = [];\n334:   for (const raw of readFileSync(inPath, 'utf8').split('\\n')) {\n335:     if (raw.trim() === '') continue;\n336:     rows.push(JSON.parse(raw));\n337:   }\n338: \n339:   const prompts = [];\n340:   for (const row of rows) {\n341:     try {\n342:       prompts.push(promptFor(row.rowId));\n343:     } catch (error) {\n344:       return refused(error?.code ?? 'unknown_row', error?.message ?? String(error));\n345:     }\n346:   }\n347: \n348:   const warmed = new Set();\n349:   const output = [];\n350:   for (let index = 0; index < rows.length; index += 1) {\n351:     const line = rows[index];\n352:     const rowId = line.rowId;\n353:     const locator = line.locator !== null && typeof line.locator === 'object' && !Array.isArray(line.locator)\n354:       ? line.locator\n355:       : null;\n356:     const projectDir = locator?.projectDir;\n357:     const checkoutDir = typeof projectDir === 'string' && projectDir !== '' ? projectDir : null;\n358: \n359:     let offer = { offeredCommand: null, offeredPolicy: null, offerError: 'no_checkout' };\n360:     if (checkoutDir !== null) {\n361:       if (!warmed.has(checkoutDir)) {\n362:         warmed.add(checkoutDir);\n363:         try {\n364:           await warm(checkoutDir);\n365:         } catch {\n366:           // A failed warm-up still lets the replay report its own miss.\n367:         }\n368:       }\n369:       offer = await replay({\n370:         checkoutDir,\n371:         prompt: prompts[index],\n372:         sessionId: `backfill-${rowId}`,\n373:       });\n374:     }\n375: \n376:     // Pi keeps its brief in a session store rather than a transcript, so the\n377:     // shared reader travels with every call. A runtime that has no reader for\n378:     // its store ignores it and answers `unknown`.\n379:     const delivered = await brief({ runtime: line.runtime, locator, loadSession: loadSharedSession });\n380: \n381:     output.push({\n382:       ...line,\n383:       offeredCommand: offer.offeredCommand ?? null,\n384:       offeredPolicy: offer.offeredPolicy ?? null,\n385:       offerError: offer.offerError ?? null,\n386:       briefDelivered: delivered,\n387:       offerBackfilled: true,\n388:     });\n389:   }\n390: \n391:   mkdirSync(dirname(outPath), { recursive: true });\n392:   writeFileSync(outPath, output.map((line) => `${JSON.stringify(line)}\\n`).join(''), 'utf8');\n393:   return { ok: true, lines: output.length, outPath };\n394: }"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/command-offer.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546178258,"end":1794546179037}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794546180570,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","part":{"id":"prt_1d349a1d9001lTSAfm5XhEW9ta","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","messageID":"msg_1d34952d6001WWg3jQehSp3lgy","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1dbf5a445cb463bcbe1d59269de9af2782015081","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":33185,"output":153,"reasoning":338,"cache":{"read":384,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794546183014,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","part":{"id":"prt_1d349ab66001ZaFQTf6FBKwpek","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","messageID":"msg_1d349a1e2001hGgRcDU3Y7arp7","type":"step-start","snapshot":"c21622b10e3c72a987bcef287a0ab028489df13b"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546198657,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","part":{"partID":"prt_1d349e699001L7g0RCYnvCI4X6","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","messageID":"msg_1d349a1e2001hGgRcDU3Y7arp7","type":"tool","id":"call_f2d647f24c504786bd91e29b","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546198169,"end":1794546198657}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546198667,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","part":{"partID":"prt_1d349e619002nJ6I0ZbDShvkVf","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","messageID":"msg_1d349a1e2001hGgRcDU3Y7arp7","type":"tool","id":"call_ffac4bf6cb18451f80a84071","tool":"skill","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"id":"sk-code"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"sk-code\">\n# Skill: sk-code\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n\n# Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n\nOne skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\nThe **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\nThe **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\nEach packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\nThis hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n`description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n`ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\nThe authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\nA packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n**Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n| **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n\n**Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n\n| Surface | Carries | Packet |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n| **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n| **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n\n**Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n- SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n- CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n- Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n\nThe hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\nAny other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n- Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n- Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n- For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n- When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n- Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\nBoth files point back here instead of restating the split.\n\nThe advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n\nA bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n- **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n- **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n- **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n- **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n- **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n- **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n- **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-code/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n  README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n  ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n  mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n  hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n  changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n  feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n  manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n  benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n  sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n  sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n  sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n  sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n  sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n  shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n```\n\nEach mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n\n### Backend\nThe `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\nIt centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\nIt carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\nThe `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\nThe `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\nThe packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\nShared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\nIt must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n\nThe **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\nTheir surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\nThose files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\nA request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\nThe acting agent applies that doctrine.\n\n### Maintenance\n\nAfter adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n- **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n- **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n- **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n- Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n- Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n- Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n- Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n- Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/findings-and-recommendations.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.json</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","title":"skill","metadata":{"metadata":{"name":"sk-code","directory":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code","truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\"sk-code\">\n# Skill: sk-code\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-code, code, surface-aware, two-axis, opencode, typescript, python, shell, json, jsonc, github-actions, ci-workflow, sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend, quality, code-review, verification, debug, implement, workflow-doctrine, mode-registry, workflowmode, packetkind, backendkind, surface-router, evidence-base, surface-packet, guard, adapter, stderr, detector -->\n\n# Code Family Hub (sk-code)\n\nOne skill has two axes, one shared surface-detection router and one advisor identity.\nThe **workflow axis** has two code modes that act: sk-code-quality and sk-code-review.\nThe **surface axis** has three read-only evidence packets the hub bundles alongside a workflow mode: sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend and sk-code-skilled.\nEach packet carries the shared **implement → debug → verify** workflow doctrine (`shared/references/workflow-*.md`, symlinked in) plus its own domain knowledge.\nThis hub holds NO per-mode logic. It routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each mode or surface keeps its own contract in its packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`.\n`description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value. Each hub-root JSON routing file therefore states the same release.\n`ROUTER.md` and `README.md` keep their own document versions, as the frontmatter-versioning standard gives every doc but `SKILL.md`.\nThe authority stops at the hub root. Each `modes[]` packet carries its own version and release cadence.\nA packet version is independent of this one by design, so a gap between them is not drift.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for code-family workflows. Invoke it as `sk-code` with an optional mode hint such as `quality: <request>`. The hub classifies the request, resolves a mode key, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n**Workflow axis** (modes that act, one of them chosen as primary):\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **sk-code-quality** | Quality gate, P0/P1/P2 author checks, comment hygiene and surface checklists. | `sk-code/sk-code-quality/` |\n| **sk-code-review** | Findings-first review, security and correctness baseline, checklists, output contract and PR-state gates. | `sk-code/sk-code-review/` |\n\n**Surface axis** (read-only, advisor-invisible evidence. When a workflow mode scores, the hub bundles the surface after that primary mode. When no workflow mode scores, the hub routes the surface alone for a lookup, implement, debug or verify task, and the acting agent hands the author gates to `sk-code-quality` before it claims completion):\n\n| Surface | Carries | Packet |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| **sk-code-ci** | GitHub Actions evidence: trigger, filter, permission, timeout, concurrency and caching rules, the protected set, the intentional message-contract exception and the scaffold templates, with the repository-specific values read from a CI profile. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-ci/` |\n| **sk-code-frontend** | Stack-agnostic frontend evidence: web markup and CSS, web layout and accessibility, visual values, visual hierarchy and review, motion and interaction, the `tokens.css` starter tokens and Figma-to-code checks. Read-only. | `sk-code/sk-code-frontend/` |\n| **sk-code-skilled** | System-code evidence: TypeScript/Python/shell/config standards, hooks, alignment verification, authoring checklists. Bundles the implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine (read-only evidence) for the Skilled surface, and the acting agent applies it. | `sk-code/sk-code-skilled/` |\n\n**Surface list.** The hub has exactly three surfaces:\n- SKILLED (`sk-code-skilled`): the native surface for this framework's own code.\n- CI (`sk-code-ci`): the GitHub Actions workflow standards surface.\n- Frontend (`sk-code-frontend`): the stack-agnostic web practice and Figma-to-code surface, with visual values, visual hierarchy and review, and motion and interaction guidance, bundled from request vocabulary alone.\n\nThe hub also has two workflow modes: quality (`sk-code-quality`) and review (`sk-code-review`).\nAny other sk-code doc that counts surfaces points to this sentence.\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Documentation-only changes with no code-work contract - use `sk-doc`.\n- Git workflow, branch, commit, PR, merge, or release work - use `sk-git`.\n- Pure browser inspection or external MCP transport work - use the relevant MCP skill.\n- For a measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source), use `sk-design-md-generator` first.\n- When a request or a frame leaves a visual value open (spacing, type, color, radius, shadow or motion), or a value sits off every token and scale, load `sk-code-frontend` and decide it from its visual-values reference.\n- Backend support primitives in `shared/` - they are consumed by the modes, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) uses `hub-router.json` as the authored mode vocabulary. `mode-registry.json` carries the packet contracts, whose aliases are checked against that vocabulary.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) uses the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) as the authored leaf-set router.\nBoth files point back here instead of restating the split.\n\nThe advisor routes every code query to the single identity `sk-code`. Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"` and follow the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default. The legacy fallback, the kill-switch and the `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST` live in the compiled-routing section of [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md).\n\nA bare implement, debug or verify request with no workflow mode becomes a pure surface bundle, and an unclear code intent asks for disambiguation. Neither path forces a stale default.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public mode or surface key a request resolves to.\n- **`packetKind`**: the axis a packet sits on, `workflow` for a mode that acts and `surface` for read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode.\n- **`backendKind`**: which backend runs the packet, `surface-router` or `review-cache` for a workflow mode and `evidence-base` for a surface.\n- **`surfaceBundle`**: the `routerPolicy.outcomes` entry where one workflow mode leads and zero or more surfaces follow as evidence.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each mode's discriminator, `surfaceDetection` markers and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's `routerSignals`, `vocabularyClasses` and the surface-bundling policy.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, plus the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list and the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble.\n- **shared control**: a hub-level resource declared in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, which resolves on disk and never projects as a leaf.\n- **leaf**: a typed `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **surface detection**: the shared detection order over the declared surfaces, which tries each specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n- **`--surface-hint`**: the front-door flag that carries the detected surface into the compiled route, so a hinted surface leads a keyword tie.\n- **`UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`**: the load level the router returns, with a disambiguation checklist, when no mode resolves.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-code/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-mode code logic)\n  README.md              # human-facing front page for the hub\n  ROUTER.md              # active stage-two surface router and shared-control declaration\n  mode-registry.json     # the two-axis discriminator + advisorRouting contract\n  hub-router.json        # lexical routing signals + surfaceBundle policy for hub-local choice\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  leaf-manifest.json     # typed (workflowMode, leaf) pairs the router guards validate\n  changelog/             # hub release notes, one v<version>.md per hub release\n  feature-catalog/       # current-state inventory of the hub's routing capabilities\n  manual-testing-playbook/  # routing and disambiguation scenarios for the hub\n  benchmark/             # historical routing benchmark inputs and reports\n  sk-code-quality/       # quality mode packet     (workflow)\n  sk-code-review/        # review mode packet      (workflow)\n  sk-code-ci/            # GitHub Actions workflow standards packet (read-only evidence; rules, protected set and scaffold templates)\n  sk-code-frontend/      # stack-agnostic web and Figma-to-code evidence packet (read-only; references/web-visual-values.md, web-visual-hierarchy.md and web-motion-and-interaction.md plus assets/tokens.css)\n  sk-code-skilled/      # skilled surface packet (read-only evidence; carries the workflow doctrine)\n  shared/                # shared surface-detection router, cross-mode helpers, and the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine (references/workflow-*.md)\n```\n\nEach mode or surface packet is self-contained and carries no per-packet `graph-metadata.json`. Only this hub carries one, so the advisor discovers exactly one code skill identity. The implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine is a single shared source under `shared/references/`, and each surface symlinks it in rather than duplicating it.\n\n### Backend\nThe `surface-router` backend is the shared surface-detection router under `shared/`.\nIt centralizes the detection order over the declared surfaces, the SKILLED fallback and UNKNOWN.\nIt carries the shared implement → debug → verify workflow doctrine consumed by each surface.\nThe `review-cache` backend supports the sk-code-review mode's non-mutating review output cache.\nThe `evidence-base` backend serves the surface packets as read-only domain evidence (`sk-code-ci/`, `sk-code-frontend/`, `sk-code-skilled/`). The hub bundles them alongside the primary mode.\nThe packet mutates nothing and never carries process. The acting agent applies the doctrine it bundles.\nShared backend material provides surface identity, the workflow doctrine and cross-mode helpers.\nIt must never gain per-mode workflow contracts.\n\nThe **implement → debug → verify** phases are not standalone modes.\nTheir surface-agnostic doctrine lives once in `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `workflow-debug.md` and `workflow-verify.md`.\nThose files are symlinked into each surface, so the active surface carries the full workflow.\nA request to implement, debug or verify code causes the router to detect its surface and load that surface's bundled doctrine.\nThe acting agent applies that doctrine.\n\n### Maintenance\n\nAfter adding or removing a surface, run `/doctor:sk-code setup`. It checks the hub and names the repair for each failing check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a mode through `mode-registry.json`, and never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep mode contracts in the mode packets, so the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one skill identity.\n- **ALWAYS** give every mode an `advisorRouting` block with `routingClass: \"metadata\"` and the correct `packetSkillName`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the implement/debug/verify workflow doctrine as one shared source under `shared/references/`, symlinked into each surface, and never fork per-surface copies.\n- **ALWAYS** keep `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` keys aligned with its intent keys and packet leaves aligned with `leaf-manifest.json`.\n- **ALWAYS** keep hub-shared map inputs explicit in `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES`, because they are control resources and never typed leaves.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a mode packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-mode quality, review, or surface workflow logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** hardcode a router mapping outside `mode-registry.json`.\n- **NEVER** reintroduce implement, debug, or verify as standalone mode packets, because they are surface-owned doctrine.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new code mode is needed - extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet. Do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- Surface identity and workflow ownership conflict in a way the shared router cannot classify.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Workflow mode packets: `sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-review/SKILL.md`.\n- Surface evidence packets: `sk-code-ci/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-frontend/SKILL.md`, `sk-code-skilled/SKILL.md`.\n- Shared workflow doctrine: `shared/references/workflow-implement.md`, `shared/references/workflow-debug.md`, `shared/references/workflow-verify.md` (symlinked into each surface).\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (two-axis: `packetKind` discriminates workflow vs surface).\n- Hub router signals + surface bundling: `hub-router.json`.\n- Surface router and shared controls: `ROUTER.md`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n- Sibling example: `.skilled/skills/sk-prompt/`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/findings-and-recommendations.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.json</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/skill-benchmark-report.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/benchmark/reports/2026-06-01--after--router/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"id\":\"sk-code\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546198041,"end":1794546198667}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794546200686,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","part":{"id":"prt_1d349f06e001v60DzilboDSsCn","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","messageID":"msg_1d349a1e2001hGgRcDU3Y7arp7","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"daed7758e783c8439c19db7e1ab6ea7d78bff32a","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":9310,"output":92,"reasoning":1909,"cache":{"read":33536,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794546203840,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","part":{"id":"prt_1d349fcc0001E5bMEHaMuIdtDW","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","messageID":"msg_1d349f077001UOsEVkLDD04Cm1","type":"step-start","snapshot":"4fd2cb42b765316fd2ecab0d02389e149589579a"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546206019,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","part":{"partID":"prt_1d34a040b001RUO7hF0QLYQ7zV","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c6f5ffey4sV46stYIlJse","messageID":"msg_1d349f077001UOsEVkLDD04Cm1","type":"tool","id":"call_e0338a2648fa4a8283e3fa78","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md, lines 1-175\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Prevent overengineering\"\n3: description: \"Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"restraint ladder\"\n6:   - \"reversal cost order\"\n7:   - \"does this need to exist\"\n8:   - \"build nothing\"\n9:   - \"future proof\"\n10:   - \"might need it later\"\n11:   - \"flexible\"\n12:   - \"best practice\"\n13:   - \"while we're here\"\n14:   - \"premature abstraction\"\n15:   - \"DRY it up\"\n16:   - \"two is not a pattern\"\n17:   - \"speculative optimization\"\n18:   - \"add a config option\"\n19:   - \"add a dependency\"\n20:   - \"feature flag\"\n21:   - \"new abstraction layer\"\n22:   - \"pre-write pass\"\n23:   - \"climbing sentence\"\n24:   - \"fallback for a constraint that does not exist\"\n25:   - \"over-engineering\"\n26: importance_tier: important\n27: contextType: reference\n28: version: 1.0.1.4\n29: ---\n30: \n31: # Rule: Prevent overengineering\n32: \n33: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before the first write of anything new.\n34: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n35: \n36: ## Fires when\n37: \n38: - Adding a file, module, class, interface, abstraction, config option, feature flag, layer, or dependency.\n39: - Writing a spec or plan whose scope adds a file, module, option, layer, or dependency, before that scope is frozen.\n40: - Generalizing something that already works for its one caller.\n41: - Writing or thinking: *flexible*, *future-proof*, *scalable*, *extensible*, *might need*, *best practice*, *while we're here*.\n42: - Adding a test beyond the coverage floor, or wrapping something already callable.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem. Build the bigger thing only\n47: after naming what fails at the smaller one.**\n48: \n49: \"It's cleaner\" is not a failure. \"A future caller might\" is not a failure. A failure\n50: is something that breaks today, for a requirement that exists today.\n51: \n52: ---\n53: \n54: ## 1. THE REVERSAL-COST ORDER\n55: \n56: Start at the cheapest move every time. **Take a costlier one only by writing the sentence\n57: that says what fails at the cheaper one** in the response, not just in your head.\n58: \n59: | Move | Cost when wrong |\n60: |------|-----------------|\n61: | **Build nothing:** existing behavior already meets the requirement | zero |\n62: | Change a value, constant, or config that exists | one line to revert |\n63: | Extend an existing function or module in place | contained to one unit |\n64: | Add a new function beside the existing ones | one new symbol to learn |\n65: | Add a new file or module | a new place readers must look |\n66: | Add an abstraction, interface, or indirection layer | every future reader pays the hop |\n67: | Add a dependency | permanent supply-chain, version and trust surface |\n68: \n69: > **This orders moves by what being wrong costs, and it is deliberately not the numbered\n70: > rung ladder.** For code, the code skill's universal quality standards are the\n71: > authoritative rungs. Route through `sk-code` to reach them. That ladder orders\n72: > *solution sources*: standard library, then native platform, then an installed\n73: > dependency. Two orderings, two axes, one authority: cite rung numbers from that file,\n74: > and cite moves by name from this one. A \"rung 2\" named here would mean something\n75: > different there.\n76: \n77: Building nothing is not a formality: a surprising share of requests are already satisfied\n78: by code that exists, and reading first is what reveals it. The sentence, written out:\n79: \n80: > \"Extending `parseConfig` in place fails, because the CLI and the daemon call it with\n81: > incompatible defaults today, so the change breaks the daemon. Adding a new function\n82: > beside it.\"\n83: \n84: If you cannot write that with a real symbol and a real caller in it, you are reaching\n85: past the move you can justify.\n86: \n87: **A short diff is not a cheaper move when the next reader has to decode it.** A one-liner that packs two decisions into one expression moves the cost to every later reader, who must rebuild the intent before they can check it. When two moves cost the same, the one that handles the edge cases correctly wins.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 2. THE PRE-WRITE PASS\n92: \n93: After reading the existing code, before the first edit, and before a spec or plan freezes its scope:\n94: \n95: 1. **Does this need to exist?** Walk §1 in order. Answer by naming the move and writing\n96:    its climbing sentence.\n97: 2. **What does it touch?** If the change can break a caller or a shared contract, name\n98:    the owning module, one real caller (`file:line`) and the contract that must not\n99:    break. Then list the tests, fixtures, config and exports the change must reach. No\n100:    real caller means the change is smaller than you think, or the code should not exist\n101:    either.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. TWO SIGNALS `AGENTS.md` DOES NOT CARRY\n106: \n107: Its Restraint Signals table binds and is not repeated here.\n108: \n109: | Signal | What it means | Do this |\n110: |--------|---------------|---------|\n111: | A config option \"so we can change it later\" | a decision deferred into a permanent branch | Hardcode it (§4) |\n112: | A wrapper that only forwards arguments | indirection with no behavior | Call the thing directly |\n113: \n114: ---\n115: \n116: ## 4. SPECIFIC RESTRAINTS\n117: \n118: **Options.** Every option is a permanent branch in the code, a row in the docs, and an\n119: axis in the test matrix. Default to hardcoding. An option earns existence when **two\n120: real callers need different values today**.\n121: \n122: **Abstraction.** One instance is a case, two a coincidence, three a pattern. Abstracting\n123: at two buys a wrong abstraction more often than it saves a duplication.\n124: \n125: **Error handling.** Catch only what you can handle. A `try` that logs and re-raises, or\n126: swallows an exception into a default, converts a loud failure into a silent wrong answer.\n127: \n128: **Defensive checks.** Do not validate what the type system, the caller contract, or the\n129: layer above already guarantees. A null check on something never null tells the next\n130: reader it *is* sometimes null.\n131: \n132: **Tests.** The coverage floor and the earns-its-place bar are `AGENTS.md` §3. The ladder\n133: applies to test code exactly as to the code under test.\n134: \n135: **Performance.** No speculative optimization. Measure under stated conditions and report\n136: baseline and delta, or leave it alone.\n137: \n138: **Fallbacks.** A second code path for a constraint the target environment does not\n139: actually have is an untested branch that will rot. Add the no-install path, the offline\n140: path, or the degraded path only when you can name the environment that needs it. \"In\n141: case\" is not an environment.\n142: \n143: **Dependencies.** Prefer what the project has. A new one also takes the\n144: `blast-radius.md` pass, because installing mutates the environment.\n145: \n146: **Moves and merges.** Moved or merged code keeps its error handling and validation. Dropping a check during a move is a behavior change that needs its own reason.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 5. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n151: \n152: Restraint constrains *how much you build*, never *how much you deliver*.\n153: \n154: - **Not a license to under-deliver.** \"This part is unnecessary\" never authorizes\n155:   cutting it. Build the frozen scope **and** raise the amendment in the same response.\n156: - **Not a reason to skip real error handling.** A failure mode that happens is not\n157:   speculative.\n158: \n159: - **Not a reason to cut accessibility.** Restraint never cuts accessibility on user-facing UI: interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state.\n160: \n161: ---\n162: \n163: ## 6. SELF-CHECK\n164: \n165: - [ ] Named the move and wrote the climbing sentence for every move past \"build nothing\".\n166: - [ ] When two moves cost the same, I took the one that handles the edge cases correctly. A shorter diff that the next reader must decode did not count as cheaper.\n167: - [ ] Every new option, abstraction and dependency has a caller that needs it **today**.\n168: - [ ] Nothing is justified only by a future nobody asked for.\n169: - [ ] If I judged part of the scope unnecessary, I built it anyway and said so.\n170: - [ ] Any fallback path I added names the environment that requires it.\n171: - [ ] Where the change touches a caller or a shared contract, I named the owner, one real caller and the contract before editing. I listed the tests, fixtures, config and exports it must reach.\n172: - [ ] Every catch handles what it catches, and no check re-validates what a type or a caller contract already guarantees.\n173: - [ ] Every performance claim carries a measurement, and every new dependency names what the project's own tools could not do.\n174: - [ ] Moved or merged code kept its error handling and validation. Every check dropped during a move has its own stated reason.\n175: - [ ] Restraint did not cut accessibility on user-facing UI, where interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\